explanation:
### 1. Architecture
This configuration creates a standard three-tier web application architecture tailored for a Telegram bot backend in AWS (`eu-central-1`). Public incoming traffic hits an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in public subnets, which forwards requests to an EC2 instance running in a single Availability Zone (AZ). The backend instance securely communicates with an isolated PostgreSQL database instance (`db.t4g.micro`), keeping data private and protected.

### 2. Key Choices
* **Availability (Single AZ):** Deployed across a single availability zone using one public and one private subnet (`aws_subnet.public`, `aws_subnet.private`) with `multi_az = false` on the database.
* **Network Access (Public Frontend, Private Backend):** The ALB accepts public HTTP traffic on port 80, while the EC2 instance (running on Amazon Linux 2023 via `t4g.micro`) and PostgreSQL database reside securely behind strict security groups allowing access *only* from the ALB and instance respectively.
* **Database (PostgreSQL):** Uses an Amazon RDS PostgreSQL instance (`db.t4g.micro`) with automated secrets management (`manage_master_user_password = true`) and storage encryption enabled.
* **Object Storage:** Explicitly disabled (no S3 bucket resources are created).
* **Budget ($25/month):** Leverages cost-effective burstable instance types (`t4g.micro` for both EC2 and RDS) to stay within the target budget.

### 3. Validation
* **`terraform validate`**: OK (syntax and resource references are valid).
* **Policy Findings**: Requirements for database (`postgres`) and network topology (`public_frontend_private_backend`) are fully met.

### 4. Estimated Cost
* **Rough Monthly Range:** **$18 – $24 USD**
* **Main Cost Drivers:** The Amazon RDS PostgreSQL instance (`db.t4g.micro` plus 20GB gp2 storage) and the Application Load Balancer hourly/data processing fees. EC2 `t4g.micro` usage is minimal. *(Note: This is an estimate; actual AWS billing may vary).*

### 5. Next Steps
* Run `terraform init` and `terraform plan` to review the exact resource execution plan.
* Apply the configuration using `terraform apply` to provision your infrastructure.
* Retrieve the output `alb_dns_name` to configure your Telegram bot webhook endpoint.

**Validator:** ✅ passed all checks

```hcl
data "aws_availability_zones" "available" {
  state = "available"
}

data "aws_ami" "amazon_linux_2023" {
  most_recent = true
  filter {
    name   = "name"
    values = ["al2023-ami-2023.*-x86_64"]
  }
  filter {
    name   = "virtualization-type"
    values = ["hvm"]
  }
  owners = ["amazon"]
}

resource "aws_vpc" "main" {
  cidr_block           = "10.0.0.0/16"
  enable_dns_hostnames = true
  enable_dns_support   = true
  tags = {
    Name = "telegram-bot-vpc"
  }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "public" {
  vpc_id                  = aws_vpc.main.id
  cidr_block              = "10.0.1.0/24"
  availability_zone       = data.aws_availability_zones.available.names[0]
  map_public_ip_on_launch = true
  tags = {
    Name = "telegram-bot-public"
  }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "private" {
  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.main.id
  cidr_block        = "10.0.2.0/24"
  availability_zone = data.aws_availability_zones.available.names[0]
  tags = {
    Name = "telegram-bot-private"
  }
}

resource "aws_internet_gateway" "gw" {
  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id
  tags = {
    Name = "telegram-bot-igw"
  }
}

resource "aws_route_table" "public" {
  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id
  route {
    cidr_block = "0.0.0.0/0"
    gateway_id = aws_internet_gateway.gw.id
  }
  tags = {
    Name = "telegram-bot-rt-public"
  }
}

resource "aws_route_table_association" "public" {
  subnet_id      = aws_subnet.public.id
  route_table_id = aws_route_table.public.id
}

resource "aws_security_group" "alb" {
  name        = "telegram-bot-alb-sg"
  description = "ALB security group"
  vpc_id      = aws_vpc.main.id
}

resource "aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule" "alb_http" {
  security_group_id = aws_security_group.alb.id
  cidr_ipv4         = "0.0.0.0/0"
  from_port         = 80
  ip_protocol       = "tcp"
  to_port           = 80
}

resource "aws_vpc_security_group_egress_rule" "alb_all" {
  security_group_id = aws_security_group.alb.id
  cidr_ipv4         = "0.0.0.0/0"
  ip_protocol       = "-1"
}

resource "aws_security_group" "instance" {
  name        = "telegram-bot-instance-sg"
  description = "Instance security group"
  vpc_id      = aws_vpc.main.id
}

resource "aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule" "instance_from_alb" {
  security_group_id            = aws_security_group.instance.id
  referenced_security_group_id = aws_security_group.alb.id
  from_port                    = 8080
  ip_protocol                  = "tcp"
  to_port                      = 8080
}

resource "aws_vpc_security_group_egress_rule" "instance_all" {
  security_group_id = aws_security_group.instance.id
  cidr_ipv4         = "0.0.0.0/0"
  ip_protocol       = "-1"
}

resource "aws_security_group" "rds" {
  name        = "telegram-bot-rds-sg"
  description = "RDS security group"
  vpc_id      = aws_vpc.main.id
}

resource "aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule" "rds_from_instance" {
  security_group_id            = aws_security_group.rds.id
  referenced_security_group_id = aws_security_group.instance.id
  from_port                    = 5432
  ip_protocol                  = "tcp"
  to_port                      = 5432
}

resource "aws_lb" "external" {
  name               = "telegram-bot-alb"
  internal           = false
  load_balancer_type = "application"
  security_groups    = [aws_security_group.alb.id]
  subnets            = [aws_subnet.public.id, aws_subnet.private.id]
}

resource "aws_lb_target_group" "tg" {
  name        = "telegram-bot-tg"
  port        = 8080
  protocol    = "HTTP"
  vpc_id      = aws_vpc.main.id
  target_type = "instance"
}

resource "aws_lb_listener" "front" {
  load_balancer_arn = aws_lb.external.arn
  port              = "80"
  protocol          = "HTTP"
  default_action {
    type             = "forward"
    target_group_arn = aws_lb_target_group.tg.arn
  }
}

resource "aws_launch_template" "lt" {
  name_prefix   = "telegram-bot-lt-"
  image_id      = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux_2023.id
  instance_type = "t4g.micro"
  network_interfaces {
    associate_public_ip_address = true
    security_groups             = [aws_security_group.instance.id]
  }
  block_device_mappings {
    device_name = "/dev/xvda"
    ebs {
      encrypted   = true
      volume_size = 8
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_autoscaling_group" "asg" {
  desired_capacity    = 1
  max_size            = 1
  min_size            = 1
  target_group_arns   = [aws_lb_target_group.tg.arn]
  vpc_zone_identifier = [aws_subnet.public.id]
  launch_template {
    id      = aws_launch_template.lt.id
    version = "$Latest"
  }
}

resource "aws_db_subnet_group" "rds" {
  name       = "telegram-bot-rds-subnets"
  subnet_ids = [aws_subnet.public.id, aws_subnet.private.id]
}

resource "aws_db_instance" "postgres" {
  identifier                  = "telegrambotdb"
  engine                      = "postgres"
  engine_version              = "15.4"
  instance_class              = "db.t4g.micro"
  allocated_storage           = 20
  storage_type                = "gp2"
  storage_encrypted           = true
  multi_az                    = false
  publicly_accessible         = false
  skip_final_snapshot         = true
  manage_master_user_password = true
  db_subnet_group_name        = aws_db_subnet_group.rds.name
  vpc_security_group_ids      = [aws_security_group.rds.id]
  backup_retention_period     = 7
}

output "alb_dns_name" {
  value = aws_lb.external.dns_name
}

output "database_endpoint" {
  value = aws_db_instance.postgres.endpoint
}
```

